  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA sharp cold front will sweep across the forecast area today.\nPrior to that, low clouds and/or fog will be prevalent early on,\nmainly on the coastal plain and central ME. \n\nThe front may be accompanied by a few gusty showers but overall\nmost locations will remain dry. The main result of the FROPA\nwill be wind as strong low level mixing will take place in the\nmidst of a tight pressure gradient. Westerly gusts of 25 to 35\nMPH continue to be expected for this afternoon, with a few\ngusts around 40 MPH possible. A few power outages will be\npossible.\n\nWinds will diminish as the night progresses tonight. However, in\nthe zones where the growing season is still active (mainly the\ncoastal plain) the winds should remain up enough to preclude a\nfrost. Therefore, no frost advisories are planned at this time.\n\nMostly sunny and dry for Tuesday except for continued gusty\nwinds of around 25 MPH.  \n\nWinds diminish Tuesday evening into Tuesday night as high pressure \nbuilds overhead. With mostly clear skies, conditions appear \nfavorable for good radiational cooling and perhaps the coldest night \nof the season thus far. Northern valleys and normally colder spots \ncould see lows in the 20s with 30s elsewhere, and frost is \nlooking likely for many areas south of the mountains with some\npossibly seeing a freeze, except maybe the immediate coast. \n\nBeyond that, the high pressure quickly moves east Wednesday, \nbringing a return to southerly flow ahead a cold front that looks to \ncross late in the Thursday or the early part of Thursday night. \nTemperatures should bounce back to near normal Wednesday (60-65F) \nsouth of the mountains and could be a few degrees above on Thursday \nwith mid 60s to around 70F. Rainfall amounts with this front \ncontinue to look light with the mountains/northern areas standing \nthe better chance for showers and light amounts. \n\nTemperatures cool back down a few degrees behind this front for \nFriday with some upslope showers possible in the mountains. Models \nare in somewhat good agreement with an upper disturbance that may \nbring a few showers Friday night into early Saturday before high \npressure brings mostly dry conditions the remainder of the weekend.",
